<?xpacket begin="ï»¿" id="W5M0MpCehiHzreSzNTczkc9d"?><x:xmpmeta xmlns:x="adobe:ns:meta/" x:xmptk="xmp-writer"><rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#"><rdf:Description rdf:about="" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/"  xmlns:xmp="http://ns.adobe.com/xap/1.0/"  xmlns:xmpMM="http://ns.adobe.com/xap/1.0/mm/"  xmlns:xmpTPg="http://ns.adobe.com/xap/1.0/t/pg/"  xmlns:pdf="http://ns.adobe.com/pdf/1.3/" ><dc:title><rdf:Alt><rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">Pupil size reflects computational processes of face recognition</rdf:li></rdf:Alt></dc:title><dc:description><rdf:Alt><rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">Pupil size is sensitive to recognition memory: previously encountered items evoke larger dilation than new items, a pattern known as the pupil old/new effect. However, pupillometric studies have mostly sorted pupil responses by accuracy, which cannot reveal which decision process the pupil reflects. Here, we combined pupillometry with evidence-accumulation modelling. Thirty-one participants completed an old/new face recognition task while their pupil size was recorded. A hierarchical linear ballistic accumulator model estimated the quality of evidence accumulation (drift rate) and response caution (threshold), which we related to mean pupil size, peak pupil size and the rate of pupil change. Correctly recognised learned faces elicited larger pupil responses than correctly rejected novel faces, and false alarms elicited larger pupils than misses, suggesting that the pupil tracks the subjective judgement of a face as â€œoldâ€. Better evidence discrimination was associated with relatively larger pupil responses for learned faces but smaller ones for novel faces, a reversal reproduced by an exploratory joint model. The pupil may therefore reflect how clearly memory evidence signals â€œoldâ€ or â€œnewâ€: a clear memory trace enlarges the pupil, whereas a clearly absent one reduces it. The threshold for â€œlearnedâ€ responses was associated only with the rate of pupil change, suggesting that pupil dynamics may track adjustments in response caution. Although modest, these associations show the added value of linking latent decision parameters to physiological markers of cognitive state, which accuracy-based analyses cannot estimate.</rdf:li></rdf:Alt></dc:description><pdf:Keywords>face perception, pupillometry, familiarity, decision models, evidence accumulation modelling, joint-modelling</pdf:Keywords><dc:creator><rdf:Seq><rdf:li>RaphaÃ«l Fournier, Paul Downing &amp; Richard Ramsey</rdf:li></rdf:Seq></dc:creator><xmp:CreatorTool>Typst 0.14.2</xmp:CreatorTool><dc:language><rdf:Bag><rdf:li>en-US</rdf:li></rdf:Bag></dc:language><xmp:ModifyDate>2026-10-09T14:53:10+02:00</xmp:ModifyDate><xmp:CreateDate>2026-10-09T14:53:10+02:00</xmp:CreateDate><xmpTPg:NPages>26</xmpTPg:NPages><dc:format>application/pdf</dc:format><xmpMM:InstanceID>obe60FHSQ1v5xEwdqILf0w==</xmpMM:InstanceID><xmpMM:DocumentID>PzexDOvAswbygO034S2QUg==</xmpMM:DocumentID><xmpMM:RenditionClass>proof</xmpMM:RenditionClass><pdf:PDFVersion>1.7</pdf:PDFVersion></rdf:Description></rdf:RDF></x:xmpmeta><?xpacket end="r"?>
endstream
